    <name>
      <text>An improper integral on a finite interval.</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Consider the integral  \(\displaystyle \int_{0}^{{@upper_lmt@}}{@func@}\)</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{0}^{{@upper_lmt@}}{@func@}=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>First, calculate the corresponding indefinite integral: </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{0}^{{@upper_lmt@}}{@func@}={@antiderivative@}\)</p>
<p>Evaluating the Definite Integral with a Limit: Since \(x=0\) is a point of divergence (the integrand goes to infinity as \(x\to 0\), this is an improper integral. We handle it by evaluating the integral from \(\in\) to {@upper_lmt@} and then taking the limit as \( \in \to 0^+\):</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{0}^{{@upper_lmt@}}{@func@}=\displaystyle \lim_{\in \to 0^+} \left[ {@antiderivative@} \right]_0^{{@upper_lmt@}}\)</p>
<p>Substitute the upper and lower limits into the antiderivative:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \lim_{\in \to 0^+} \left( ({@upper_limit@})-({@lower_limit@}) \right)\)</p>
<p>\(\quad \quad \quad= {@ta1@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
